\title{Memory Checking in zkVMs}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Random-access memory is the hardest part of a zkVM to constrain and the most common source of
    unsoundness. The general machinery is in [permutation and memory arguments](dhsorens-002X); this
    page is about what the systems actually do.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Read-write RAM by offline memory checking, as in SP1, Jolt, and RISC Zero.}
        \li{Paged memory with Merkle verification, which RISC Zero also supports, and when that is
        preferable.}
        \li{Stack-based models (Miden, zkWasm) and how their consistency arguments differ.}
        \li{Memory across [shard boundaries](dhsorens-003O) — the argument that ties independent
        segments together.}
        \li{The concrete cost of a load or store, counted in trace cells and lookups.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [SoK: Understanding zkVM](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/525),
    [Nebula](https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1605).
}
